  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Prioritizing UI Simplicity**\n\nI\'m focusing on the technical stack for a vulnerabilities tracking and notification application. React with NextUI seems like a solid, efficient choice for the front end, particularly given the need for a professional look and feel. The Service Level Agreement (SLA) component is out. My priority is a clean, automated UI without distracting aesthetics.\n\n\n**Refining UI Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the UI aesthetics, specifically focusing on achieving a clean, professional look and feel. I\'m exploring whether to use `@nextui-org/react` (now `@heroui/react`) directly or recreate its design system with Tailwind classes. My focus is on achieving the same sleek, minimalist style with subtle borders, refined card surfaces, muted accents, clean typography, badge chips, and smooth tabs. I am verifying its installability and speed relative to rebuilding from Tailwind. Also, I\'ve removed all SLA-related elements from the header.\n\n\n**Defining Core Automation**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the application\'s core automation. The key is zero human intervention. This means an automated, unattended pipeline: a scheduled ingestion, automated CPE matching, deduplication and enrichment of CVEs, and immediate notification dispatch as soon as a match is detected, all without manual input. I\'m focusing on the \"set it and forget it\" workflow, now that I\'ve settled the UI.\n\n\n**Refining Alerting Mechanism**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the alerting mechanism and have decided on distribution lists or webhooks, with no manual approvals. The goal is an autonomous pipeline engine with event streams, auto-dispatch triggers, a dead-letter quarantine, and a comprehensive audit log. I am also prioritizing a clean, subdued, enterprise-grade dark palette for the UI, ditching the flashy neon colors, blinking borders, and pulsating animations. I want deep neutral backgrounds and crisp, subtle text hierarchy, with muted semantic colors and clean NextUI elements.\n\n\n**Evaluating UI Implementation**\n\nI\'m now investigating potential React 19 compatibility issues with NextUI (now HeroUI). It seems that using the library directly may introduce peer dependency conflicts. I\'m exploring the feasibility of recreating the NextUI design components natively with Tailwind to guarantee build reliability and avoid these errors. The UI will focus on crisp minimalism with a neutral, enterprise-grade dark aesthetic.\n\n\n**Streamlining Alert Data**\n\nI\'m now streamlining the data structure for alerts, focusing on key elements like severity, exploit risk, and automation status, and removing legacy SLA fields. I\'m focusing on the pipeline engine and will work on cleaning up the seed data to reflect a zero-touch pipeline. I\'m committed to maintaining strict CPE matching and canonical CVE deduplication, so these features will remain.\n\n\n**Implementing Autonomous Triage**\n\nI\'m now implementing autonomous triage. I\'ve removed the `classifyPriorityAndSla` and SLA logic. The focus is now on an autonomous pipeline with ingestion, normalization, enrichment, product watchlist CPE matching, automated risk assessment, and instant notification dispatch, all without human intervention. I am implementing automated pipeline runners with a notification queue, cleaned up state, and actions. Also, I am refactoring the UI to the NextUI / HeroUI design system, replacing all SLA-related views.\n\n\n**Defining Core Components**\n\nI\'m now defining the core components for the application\'s functionality. I\'m focusing on the product-affected column, CVE details, automated remediation steps, and notification history. I\'m building out components like a threat data view, advisories view, pipeline executions view, and alerted CVEs view. This will include threat feeds, recipients view, and audit logs.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
