  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Inspecting The Build**\n\nI\'ve got a successful build, so I\'m now meticulously examining the files. My primary focus is on removing the Service Level Agreement (SLA) completely. The user\'s directive is clear: eliminate it, as this application tracks product vulnerabilities and sends automated notifications. I\'m prioritizing this refactor to ensure it aligns with the user\'s specific request.\n\n\n**Refining Application Autonomy**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the application\'s autonomous capabilities. The goal is to ensure it operates entirely without human intervention. The earlier focus on SLA removal has paved the way. I\'m building out logic that centers on autonomous vulnerability tracking and alerting. My new focus is now on ensuring it can ingest, normalize, and match vulnerabilities, route notifications, and dispatch bulletins seamlessly. This includes considering autonomous agent engine status, auto-dispatch rules, and noise reduction.\n\n\n**Enhancing Matching Capabilities**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in implementing improvements to the enterprise-grade matching engine. I\'m focusing on dual parsing and normalization for CPE 2.3 and 2.2 formats, especially WFN and URI formats. I\'m building version range evaluations with SemVer, covering all expressions, handling vendor patch releases, and adding Package URL (PURL) support. I\'m also including vendor and product aliases and synonyms. The next task involves considering the product asset hierarchy and the blast radius of matching components.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Confidence & Tracing**\n\nI\'ve shifted my attention to refining the matching engine\'s confidence scoring and providing detailed match reasons. I\'m implementing a tiered confidence system, ranging from a precise CPE 2.3 match at 100% to substring-based matches with version heuristics at 70%. Each match will include a trace, showing the rule or CPE/PURL that triggered it.\n\nI\'m now integrating an autonomous de-noising and false-positive suppression engine. This will use architecture/OS filtering and component status to suppress false positives. I\'m also including known false-positive suppression rules with audit logging. The focus is on a clean, professional user interface, inspired by NextUI, using dark enterprise themes with zinc and slate tones.\n\n\n"
