  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Envisioning Enterprise Matching**\n\nI\'m now picturing an \"Enterprise Matching Inspector & Simulator\" as a tab or drawer within `WatchListView.tsx`, or perhaps as a standalone tool! I\'m leaning towards the former as it seems more streamlined. The user\'s directive is driving this design, and it\342\200\231s become clear that enterprise-grade enhancements are crucial for matching improvements and other core areas. I\'m focusing on the implementation!\n\n\n**Analyzing Matching Requirements**\n\nI\'m deep-diving into the specific enterprise enhancements needed, particularly for the matching engine. Multi-vector matching is a key area, focusing on CPE, PURL, semantic versioning, vendor aliases, and environment filtering. I\'m prioritizing the implementation of these components to create a robust and precise matching system. The need for VEX integration is clear as well.\n\n\n**Developing Matching Insights**\n\nI\'m now outlining key functionalities: a match trace with explanatory audits, outputting the exact matching criteria, and an interactive engine simulator for real-time CVE matching. This tool allows security engineers to input assets and immediately see matches, confidence scores, and suppression details. I\'m focusing on the integration of this interactive \"Engine Lab\" and the \"Autonomous Alerting & Notification\" flow.\n\n\n**Implementing Alert Dispatches**\n\nI\'m now focusing on automating alert dispatches. This includes role-based email filtering (CISA, critical, all severities), webhook integrations with Slack, Teams, and SIEM/SOAR systems. I\'m prioritizing batching low-severity alerts into digests and de-noising the output through CVE-ID normalization and duplicate suppression. The goal is to avoid alert fatigue!\n\n\n"
